{
  "abstract": "For every prime $p \\equiv 3 \\pmod 4$ with $p \\ge 7$ we construct explicit balanced metacyclic Kummer families of abelian varieties of Weil type over $K = \\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{-p})$, of unbounded dimension, with non-split discriminant and with algebraic Weil classes on every member. The conductor is $m = \\ell p$ for an auxiliary prime $\\ell \\equiv 1 \\pmod 4$ inert in $K$ with $\\gcd(\\ell-1, (p-1)/2) = 1$; such $\\ell$ exist for every $p$ by Dirichlet's theorem, and $\\ell = 5$ works when $p \\equiv 3, 7 \\pmod{20}$. The key observation is that the Fermat characters carrying the Weil classes are joins of Aoki-Shioda $\\ell$-standard characters of degree $\\ell p$ and linear characters, which are algebraic by explicit constructions; no case of the Hodge conjecture for Fermat varieties beyond these classical cycles is needed, and balance follows from the same decomposition. The discriminant class is $[\\ell]$, with local obstructions exactly at $\\ell$ and $p$; it comes from evaluating an earlier closed formula at conductors $\\ell p$ with $K = \\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{-p})$, which also shows that, under its hypotheses, non-split data need $\\ell \\equiv 1 \\pmod 4$. Algebraicity and non-splitness alone are weak, since products of a non-split Weil fourfold with suitable split CM factors have them; the point is that these families can have full monodromy. For $\\ell = 5$ and $\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{-7})$ in dimensions $34, 46, 58$ (a Lie-algebra span) and $\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{-23})$ in dimension $130$ (a transvection-group certificate with Kantor's theorem), exact computations show that the complexified connected monodromy is $\\mathrm{SL}_n$. So very general members have Hodge group $\\mathrm{SU}(q,q)$ and endomorphism algebra $K$, and the Hodge and generalized Hodge conjectures hold for all their powers. Uniform monodromy is open. The period images are low-dimensional; nothing is claimed about general members of the ambient Weil moduli spaces.",
